# apps/ui/.env — OPTIONAL. You do not need this file to run Kermanych.
#
# Out of the box the ui talks to the team's hosted Supabase project, which is
# compiled into `packages/cloud` as the built-in default. Copy this file to
# `.env` (gitignored) only when you want to point the ui somewhere ELSE: a local
# Supabase stack or your own fork of the project.
#
# Set BOTH lines or neither. Exactly one of them is a configuration error, not a
# fallback: a custom URL with the team's key (or the reverse) points at a backend
# that cannot answer, so `cloudEnv('ui')` refuses with `cloud env missing: …`.
# Whatever you set here must also be set for the API — see `SUPABASE_URL` /
# `SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY` in the README — or the two halves of the app end up
# on different backends.
#
# Both values are PUBLIC: the publishable key ships inside the browser bundle by
# design, and RLS is the authorization surface. Paste them from `supabase status`
# (local stack) or from your project's Settings → API keys.
#
# The dashboard's `Publishable key` (`sb_publishable_…`) is the value that used to
# be called `anon`. A local Supabase CLI stack still hands out the legacy `anon`
# JWT as well (`supabase status` labels them ANON_KEY / PUBLISHABLE_KEY), so both
# shapes are normal on one machine; either works here. If both names are set, the
# publishable one is used.
#
# NEVER put the dashboard's `Secret key` (`sb_secret_…`, formerly `service_role`)
# in this file or in any other environment variable: Kermanych never reads it.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54421
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=paste-the-publishable-key-here
# Legacy name, still accepted (this is what a local `supabase status` prints):
# VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=paste-the-anon-key-here
